<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Kivu</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Kivu is one of the African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a> on the border between Rwanda and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>. It drains south through the Ruzizi river into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, and its deep waters hold large amounts of dissolved methane. The Kingdom of Rwanda took shape in the hills to its east, and Goma, Bukavu and Gisenyi stand on its shores.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-kivu.jpg" alt="Lake Kivu" width="296" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LakeKivu_satellite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Kivu lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-rift">Albertine Rift</a>, the western branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">East African Rift</a>, on the border between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and Rwanda. It is about 42 kilometres long and 50 kilometres at its widest, with a surface at 1,460 metres above sea level. Its maximum depth is 475 metres. The lake bed lies on a rift valley that is slowly being pulled apart, which causes volcanic activity in the area. About 58 percent of its waters lie within the borders of the Democratic Republic of the Congo. Settlements on its shore include Bukavu, Kabare, Kalehe, Sake and Goma on the Congolese side and Gisenyi, Kibuye and Cyangugu in Rwanda.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the past Lake Kivu drained north and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Nile</a>. About 13,000 to 9,000 years ago volcanic activity blocked its outlet to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> watershed and raised mountains, including the Virungas, between Lake Kivu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-edward">Lake Edward</a>. The water was then forced south down the Ruzizi River. This in turn raised the level of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, which overflowed down the Lukuga River.</p><p>Researchers have found evidence around the lake of massive local extinctions about every thousand years, presumably caused by outgassing events. Its fish fauna is relatively poor, with 28 described species. In late 1959 the Belgian agronomist Alphonse Collart introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a> sardine to fill an empty niche, since no fish fed on plankton in the open water. The sardine has since gained substantial economic and nutritional importance for people on the shore.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lake Kivu is meromictic: a freshwater surface layer becomes more saline with depth. With Lake Nyos and Lake Monoun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> it is one of three lakes known to undergo limnic eruptions, in which the overturn of deep layers releases dissolved carbon dioxide. Its bottom also holds methane, estimated at 65 cubic kilometres, produced by microbial reduction of volcanic carbon dioxide, and about two million people live in the lake basin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> word mazuku, evil wind, names the outgassing of methane and carbon dioxide that kills people and animals. Methane has been drawn from the lake for a brewery at Gisenyi and for electricity generation in Rwanda.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Kivu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Rwanda took shape in the hills east of <b>Lake Kivu</b>, among farming and herding communities that shared a single language, Kinyarwanda, and clans that cut across the groups later classed as Hutu, Tutsi and Twa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
